/// \defgroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_a Behavior modules and their factory functions (A to M)
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Sixteen of BFME 2's behavior module classes, from
///        AimWeaponBehavior to MineshaftPortalBehaviour, the instance
///        factory each one registers with the module factory, and the INI
///        data factories of nine of their module data classes.
///
/// A behavior module gives an object one piece of game behavior: a castle
/// that unpacks, a gate that opens, a death sequence, a hero announcement.
/// Object INI lists it on a module line (`Behavior =
/// GateOpenAndCloseBehavior ModuleTag_...`); the tag after the name is the
/// author's choice and identifies the module within its object
/// (\ref page_modding_modules). This group documents the classes at the
/// level of their role and INI contract, and the code that creates the
/// modules and their data. The modules' own update, damage and death logic
/// is separate reverse-engineering work and is not described here. BFME 2
/// registers many other behavior modules; only the classes listed below are
/// covered.
///
/// \par The factory flow
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance
/// factory, a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the
/// registrations are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`.
/// The data factory runs while INI loads (ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI),
/// the instance factory when an object is built (ModuleFactory::newModule).
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories describes this flow step
/// by step and \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories the shared code
/// shape of both factories; both apply here. Neither factory is called
/// directly: the module factory reaches them only through the registered
/// pointers.
///
/// \par Registrations
/// - Every name in this group is spelled exactly like its class and is
///   registered as a logic (behavior) module. Three names use the British
///   spelling "Behaviour": DynamicPortalBehaviour,
///   FakePathfindPortalBehaviour and MineshaftPortalBehaviour. INI must use
///   the registered spelling.
/// - Read with Zero Hour's bit values (`Common/Module.h`), the interface
///   masks are: update only for AimWeaponBehavior, BuildingBehavior,
///   ClickReactionBehavior, DualWeaponBehavior, EnragedBehavior,
///   GettingBuiltBehavior and HitReactionBehavior; update and die for the
///   two slow-death classes; update and create for CastleBehavior; damage
///   for CastleMemberBehavior; create and die for
///   AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior; update and collide for
///   GateOpenAndCloseBehavior; update, die and create for
///   FakePathfindPortalBehaviour and MineshaftPortalBehaviour; and die,
///   damage, create and upgrade for DynamicPortalBehaviour. The BFME 2
///   meaning of the bits is not confirmed here, but
///   AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior, whose onCreate and onDie bodies are a
///   creation step and a death step, fits the Zero Hour reading.
/// - Seven of the sixteen register a data factory that is not documented
///   in this group: AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior, CastleBehavior,
///   EnragedBehavior, GettingBuiltBehavior and the three portal classes.
///   Their class pages say what is known about their data.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory here has the same frame: allocate the class's
///   size with the global \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory), run the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data inside an
///   exception frame that frees the block if the constructor throws, and
///   return the result as a Module pointer. When the allocation returns
///   null the constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - GateOpenAndCloseBehavior is the one exception in shape: its Module
///   base is not at the start of the object, so its factory adjusts the
///   new pointer to that base before returning it, keeping null as null.
/// - Every data factory here has one frame as well: allocate, run the data
///   class's default constructor inside an exception frame, then, when the
///   INI pointer is not null, call INI::initFromINIMultiProc with the new
///   data and the class's static \c buildFieldParse. Parsing happens after
///   the exception frame has closed, so an INI error thrown while parsing
///   does not free the new data. The parse step does not test the
///   allocation: a null block with a non-null INI pointer would still be
///   handed to the parser. Zero Hour's data factory macro has the same
///   order.
/// - Seven of the nine \c buildFieldParse functions add one table of the
///   class's own fields and nothing else. The two slow-death data classes
///   first run SlowDeathBehaviorModuleData::buildFieldParse and then add
///   their own table, so their blocks accept three tables. The shared step
///   adds two:
///   - the slow-death table: \c SinkRate, \c ProbabilityModifier,
///     \c ModifierBonusPerOverkillPercent, \c SinkDelay,
///     \c SinkDelayVariance, \c DestructionDelay,
///     \c DestructionDelayVariance, \c DecayBeginTime, \c FX, \c OCL,
///     \c Weapon, \c Sound, \c FlingForce, \c FlingForceVariance,
///     \c FlingPitch, \c FlingPitchVariance, \c DeathFlags,
///     \c ShadowWhenDead, \c FadeDelay and \c FadeTime;
///   - a table of death conditions: \c DeathTypes, \c ExemptStatus,
///     \c RequiredStatus, \c DamageAmountRequired, \c MinKillerAngle and
///     \c MaxKillerAngle. Zero Hour: the corresponding table is DieMuxData's,
///     which there holds \c DeathTypes, \c VeterancyLevels,
///     \c ExemptStatus and \c RequiredStatus; the BFME 2 table has no
///     \c VeterancyLevels entry.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template.
///   It is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from the
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Origins
/// None of the sixteen classes exists in Zero Hour. Open-BFME-1's \c game/
/// tree has code for most of them, so those classes already existed in
/// BFME 1; it has none for AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior,
/// FakePathfindPortalBehaviour or MineshaftPortalBehaviour.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags and each
///   class's fields. The field lists on the class pages are read from the
///   retail field tables, so they are complete for the tables they name.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names and what each class does with its
///   fields. A new kind of behavior needs a new class and a new
///   ModuleFactory::init registration (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Multiplayer: these are logic modules and their data comes from
///   \c Object blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Duration fields read by INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt (for example
/// \c HitReactionLifeTimer1, \c MinimumSwitchTime, \c ResetTimeInMilliseconds
/// and \c CrewPrepareTime) are written in milliseconds and turned into whole
/// logic frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details).
/// Fields read as plain integers or reals, such as \c ClickReactionTimer,
/// \c ReactionFrames1 and \c RebuildTimeSeconds, are not converted by
/// the parser; how the consuming code treats their units is not described
/// here. A change to the logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop) therefore
/// affects the two kinds of field differently.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class AimWeaponBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_a
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "AimWeaponBehavior": an update
///        module configured by aim thresholds and near and far distances.
///
/// Its data class reads four reals: \c AimLowThreshold,
/// \c AimHighThreshold, \c AimNearDistance and \c AimFarDistance. The
/// names suggest that the module relates the way its object aims a weapon
/// to the distance of the target; this page does not confirm that, and the
/// per-frame logic that reads the fields is not described here. BFME 1
/// already has the class.
///
/// \see AimWeaponBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      AimWeaponBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class AimWeaponBehaviorModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_a
/// \brief Module data for AimWeaponBehavior: the four aim fields.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// AimWeaponBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template.

/// \fn Module *AimWeaponBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AimWeaponBehavior": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AimWeaponBehaviorModuleData, shared
///                    with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AimWeaponBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *AimWeaponBehaviorModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "AimWeaponBehavior": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming the module. The group page describes the
/// shared frame.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see AimWeaponBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_behavior_module_factories_a
/// \brief Behavior module registered as "AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior":
///        reports its object's creation and death to the in-game UI as
///        hero events.
///
/// The module has a creation step and a death step. Each checks that the
/// in-game UI (\c TheInGameUI) exists and that the module has an object,
/// then passes the object to a small in-game UI routine. The creation
/// routine names the event "HeroInitialSpawn" and the death routine
/// "HeroDeath"; both hand the event name, the object and two settings held
/// by the in-game UI to one shared hero-notification routine of the UI,
/// which this page does not describe.
///
/// Its registered data factory builds a plain module data whose parse step
/// adds no fields, so the module's INI block takes no fields of its own.
/// The class is not in Zero Hour, and Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has no
/// code for it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Adding the module to an object is the whole configuration: the object
/// then raises the two hero events above. What the player sees comes from
/// the in-game UI, not from this module.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// This is a logic module that calls straight into a client-side
/// subsystem (\ref sub_gameclient) on creation and death. A remaster that
/// separates the game logic from the client, or runs it without one,
/// meets this seam here; the null check on the UI is what lets the logic
/// run without it.
///
/// \see AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared frame.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AnnounceBirthAndDeathBehavior


